A single POSIX shell script providing the ergonomics layer over the format.
Not a runtime dependency: every command must be something a person could
have done by hand with mkdir, mv, and an editor, leaving the repository
byte-identical.

The one exception is claiming, which needs the lock in 8 to be safe under
concurrent agents. Everything else stays hand-doable.

Subcommands are broken out as child tickets. 13 is the entry point for a
project that has no tracker yet.

Done. Every subcommand is implemented and closed under this ticket: new (7),
claim/release and the lock (8), list/tree/check (9), edit (10), reopen (11),
reap (12), init (13), handoff (14), and two that came out of building them —
test concurrency as a machine property (15) and closed-ness matched relative
to .pidly (22). close came in with 11, since they are one move each way.

The acceptance test held for all of them: every command is something a
person could have done with mkdir, mv and an editor, leaving the repository
byte-identical. Claiming is the exception the ticket allowed for, and it is
the only thing behind the lock.

1655 lines of POSIX shell, 12 suites, 258 cases. What is left is the
workflow around the script rather than the script: 6, 17 and 23.
